# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and on checkout.
#
# Not a style preference. This project builds and tests on Windows *and* Linux
# in CI, and the default Windows behaviour (checkout as CRLF) means the same file
# hashes differently on the two platforms. That produces phantom diffs, breaks
# `format:check` on one runner but not the other, and — the reason it matters
# here — makes a source file's published hash depend on which machine touched it
# last. A project whose whole argument is "verify what we published" cannot have
# that.
#
# Set before the first commit deliberately: correcting line endings afterwards
# rewrites every file in one enormous diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets, never normalised. Icons live in build/ per §10.2.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Lockfiles are generated. Collapsing them in reviews keeps a dependency change
# readable — the diff that matters is package.json, and the lockfile is the
# consequence.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff

# The spike is reference code, not shipped. Excluding it from language stats
# stops GitHub reporting the repository as being mostly Phase 0 CLI.
spike/** linguist-vendored=true
